THE RESIDUAL HOLE, AS A FIXTURE.  Currently expected 2; the point is the third
line of the classifier's output, not the exit code.

    SetWindowTextW(hwnd, alreadyFreedString)

Use site: user32, because the OS is the thing dereferencing the pointer.  Free
site: the IME, because it is the thing that ran the free.  Both third-party, so
the two RESPONSIBILITY stacks both say "not ours" and the report is filed
[known] -- while the string itself came out of OUR operator new, in our Label
constructor.  Memory we allocated, that something dangles into, is our defect
to fix no matter whose finger was on the trigger.

That is what the OWNERSHIP judgment is for, and what this fixture pins.  In
phase A the classifier must print

    alloc: ... geeyoou::Label::Label  [Ours]  ...\src\widget\Label.cpp
    ^ SHADOW: phase B would call this report OURS (allocation-site owner).

and must STILL exit 2.  If the exit code here ever becomes 1 without the phase
B evidence in the header of classify-asan.ps1 having been collected first,
somebody promoted a shadow judgment on a hunch.

Note the allocation stack has exactly the same plumbing in front of it as the
free stack does -- gyAllocate / operator new, both in tests\framework\Test.cpp.
If the plumbing skip were not applied to the allocation stack too, EVERY report
in the binary would show alloc: Test.cpp [Ours] and this judgment would carry
no information at all.  Compare third-party-sogou-uaf.log, where the identical
plumbing is crossed and the answer comes out SogouPY.ime: that is the proof the
ownership rule discriminates rather than just always saying "ours".

